# Vercel Speed Insights Implementation

## Overview
Successfully implemented Vercel Speed Insights in the React + Vite application as requested.

## Changes Made

### Modified Files:
1. **src/App.jsx**
   - Added import for `SpeedInsights` component from `@vercel/speed-insights/react`
   - Wrapped the existing `<Routes>` component with a React Fragment (`<>`)
   - Added `<SpeedInsights />` component at the bottom of the App component
   - This ensures Speed Insights tracks all routes in the application

2. **package-lock.json**
   - Updated lockfile after running `npm install` to ensure dependencies are properly resolved
   - No new dependencies added (package already had `@vercel/speed-insights` v1.3.1)

## Implementation Details

The implementation follows Vercel's official documentation for React applications:
- Used the `@vercel/speed-insights/react` package (already installed as a dependency)
- Placed the `<SpeedInsights />` component in the root App component to track all pages
- The component is rendered alongside the Routes to ensure it's present on all pages

## Verification

✅ **Build Status**: Successfully built with `npm run build`
- No build errors introduced
- Build output: 559.01 kB main bundle (gzipped: 154.85 kB)

✅ **Linting**: No new linting errors introduced
- Pre-existing linting issues remain unchanged
- Changes to App.jsx are clean and follow React best practices

## Next Steps

To enable Speed Insights data collection:
1. Deploy the application to Vercel
2. Enable Speed Insights in the Vercel dashboard (Project → Speed Insights tab)
3. After deployment, verify the `/_vercel/speed-insights/script.js` is loaded
4. Performance metrics will begin appearing in the dashboard after users visit the site

## Notes

- The `@vercel/speed-insights` package was already installed in package.json
- No additional configuration is required
- The component will automatically track Core Web Vitals and performance metrics
- Data will only be collected in production deployments on Vercel
